  "target": {
    "task": "The City of Khulna runs an open data portal. Four staff publish datasets that nine city departments send in at irregular times, and users complain that they never know when new data will appear. Plan the portal's release process for 2027.",
    "rules": [
      {
        "id": "R1",
        "text": "cadence_weeks is the planned number of weeks between releases, a whole number from 2 to 13.",
        "checks": [
          {
            "id": "R1a",
            "spec": [
              "json_all_int",
              "cadence_weeks"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "R1b",
            "spec": [
              "json_all_ge",
              "cadence_weeks",
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "R1c",
            "spec": [
              "json_all_le",
              "cadence_weeks",
              13
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "R2",
        "text": "if_dataset_not_ready is ship_without_it or delay_release.",
        "checks": [
          {
            "id": "R2a",
            "spec": [
              "json_in",
              "if_dataset_not_ready",
              [
                "ship_without_it",
                "delay_release"
              ]
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "R3",
        "text": "releases_in_2027 is between 4 and 26.",
        "checks": [
          {
            "id": "R3a",
            "spec": [
              "json_all_ge",
              "releases_in_2027",
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "R3b",
            "spec": [
              "json_all_le",
              "releases_in_2027",
              26
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "R4",
        "text": "List 2 to 4 stages a dataset passes through before release.",
        "checks": [
          {
            "id": "R4a",
            "spec": [
              "json_len_between",
              "stages",
              2,
              4
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "format": "Reply with JSON only, in this shape: {\"cadence_weeks\": 6, \"if_dataset_not_ready\": \"ship_without_it or delay_release\", \"releases_in_2027\": 8, \"stages\": [\"...\"]}"
  },
  "source_example": {
    "text": "In 2011 Mozilla moved Firefox from a release every year or so to a rapid release cycle of six weeks. Firefox 5, shipped in June 2011, was the first version made under the new process. New code entered a channel called Nightly, moved after six weeks to Aurora, then to Beta, and finally to Release. Every six weeks each channel moved up one step, like carriages on a train that leaves on time. A feature that was not ready simply missed the train and waited for the next one, so the release date never moved. Release managers could still approve small, urgent fixes for a version already in Beta; these requests were called uplifts. The aim was to get finished work to users quickly and to make release dates predictable for users and add-on developers. Some enterprise customers complained that the fast pace left them no time to test, and Mozilla added an Extended Support Release in 2012. Google Chrome had used a six-week schedule since 2010; Firefox moved to a four-week cycle in 2020 and Chrome followed in 2021. Mozilla later replaced the Aurora channel with Developer Edition. The approach is now widely known as the release train model.",
    "length": "standard",
    "incidental_details": [
      {
        "id": "I1",
        "text": "Channel names Nightly and Aurora",
        "leak_terms": [
          "Nightly",
          "Aurora"
        ],
        "control": {
          "from": "Aurora",
          "to": "Comet",
          "leak_terms": [
            "Nightly",
            "Comet"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "I2",
        "text": "Uplift requests",
        "leak_terms": [
